INTRODUCTORY NOTE TO RULE 12
Rule 12 states how a defendant may seek dismissal of the plaintiff’s complaint or ask the court to require the plaintiff to amend the complaint. So far in this chapter, we have encountered Rule 12 in the context of motions under Rule 12(b)(6) to dismiss for failure to state a claim upon which relief may be granted. A Rule 12(b)(6) motion is the primary way that a defendant can seek dismissal of a complaint that fails to satisfy the Rule 8(a)(2) pleading standard, but Rule 12 places several more weapons in the defendant’s arsenal.

Rules 12(b)(1)–(5) and 12(b)(7) allow a defendant to move to dismiss based on deficiencies in the action other than the complaint’s failure to state a claim. We will set them aside for the remainder of this chapter, but each one of these grounds for dismissal of the action is the subject of discussion elsewhere in this book.*

Apart from Rule 12(b), Rule 12 provides for motions for judgment on the pleadings under Rule 12(c), motions for a more definite statement under Rule 12(e), and motions to strike under Rule 12(f). As you read Rule 12, think
