NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON RULE 12 MOTIONS
1. Rule 12(c) allows a party to move for judgment on the pleadings “[a]fter the pleadings are closed.” Pleadings are considered closed once all pleadings presenting claims (including counterclaims and the like) and all responsive pleadings (answers and replies) have been filed and served. The standard for deciding a Rule 12(c) motion is the same as that for deciding a Rule 12(b)(6) motion. See Hogan v. Fischer, 738 F.3d 509, 514–15 (2d Cir. 2013). But unlike a Rule 12(b)(6) motion, all of the pleadings may be considered, not just the complaint. That said, if the court considers matters outside the pleadings, then the motion is treated as one for summary judgment (addressed in Chapter 6), wherein the parties can present evidence outside the pleadings and which is subject to entirely different rules.

Why might a defendant file a motion for judgment on the pleadings rather than a motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim? [Hint: the principal differences between these motions are when they are filed and what pleadings the court can consider in deciding them.]
